- 1. **`agentRole`** — coarse-grained role label (`CEO`, `Researcher`, `Developer`, `Designer`, `Marketer`, `Operator`, etc.). Best signal for "which agent is the planner?" or "which agent writes code?".
- 2. **`tags[]` + `capabilities[]`** — finer-grained markers the user set on the agent (e.g. `tags: ["video"]` or `capabilities: ["copywriting"]`).
- 3. **`tools[]`** — which tools the agent has enabled. If a subtask needs `Design_Agent` to run, prefer an agent that already has it (otherwise you'll have to override with `attachedToolNames`).
- 4. **`name` / `description`** — last-resort fuzzy match if roles/tags are too generic.
- 5. **`status: "active"`** — never pick an `inactive` or `archived` agent.
- `workstationKey` is the planner's hint about what kind of work the step needs:
- | workstationKey | Suggested agent role            |
- | -------------- | ------------------------------- |
- | `researching`  | Researcher / Analyst / Data     |
- | `working`      | Developer / Designer / Operator |
- | `syncing`      | Operator / Integrator           |
- | `error`        | Reserved — don't assign here    |
- Use `workstationKey` as your first signal when matching agents.
- 4. GET /api/missions/<id>/subtasks → confirm IDs + workstationKeys
